 ## Search order, mandatory
 
-The repo carries a `graft/` graph. Use it before any text search or file
-read; it is the cheap path and the exhaustive one.
-
-- `graft ask "<question>" --source` to locate and understand.
-- `graft callers <symbol> --depth all` for who depends on a symbol.
-- `graft grep "<literal>"` for every occurrence, grouped by symbol; it is
-  faster than `rg` and it is the blast-radius list.
-- `graft skeleton <file>` for a file's surface instead of reading it.
-- `Read` with a line range only for the span graft named. Never a whole
-  file. Never `sed -n` one range per turn.
-- `rg` only for prose, config, schema strings, YAML and docs, which the
-  graph does not index.
-
-If the worktree has no `graft/` graph, stop and say so; a read-only gate
-returns RED with that single blocker, a coder asks the coordinator to
-build it. Do not fall back to text search. Batch: one shell call carries
-several questions. Every turn re-reads your whole context, so the cost of
-a pre-flight or a review is the number of turns, not the size of the
-report.
+Use CodeGraph first for indexed code, with your absolute worktree on every
+call: CLI `-p <wt>`, MCP `projectPath: "<wt>"`. A subagent shares its
+parent's connection; its own worktree must be explicit.
+
+- `codegraph explore <SymA> <SymB> -p <wt>` returns source and call paths.
+- `codegraph callers SYMBOL -p <wt>` and `impact SYMBOL -p <wt>` map reach.
+- `codegraph grep <pattern> -p <wt>` groups text hits by enclosing symbol.
+- `codegraph node -f <file> --symbols-only -p <wt>` shows a file's API.
+- Returned source is already read. Read only an uncovered span or a file
+  flagged as changed on disk. Use `rg` for prose, config and string uses.
+
+There is no build step. If an index is building or copying, retry shortly.
+Never run `init`, `index`, `uninit`, `unlock`, `install`, `uninstall`,
+`upgrade` or `telemetry`; indexing a new repository is the user's decision.
+
+**Request gate:** `codegraph checkpoint --fresh <wt>`, then `codegraph blast
+-b <base-ref> -p <wt>` (or `impact SYMBOL -p <wt> --fresh` before editing).
+**Pre-push gate:** fresh checkpoint, then `codegraph orphans -b <base-ref>
+-p <wt>`, followed by `rg -nw SYMBOL` for dynamic/string uses of candidates.
+Record checkpoint HEAD, working-tree hash and pending files: the wait is
+bounded; pending files mean incomplete freshness, not a passing gate.
+MCP gates carry `projectPath`, `base` and `fresh: true`. CLI blast/orphans
+use the explicit checkpoint, not a `--fresh` flag.
 
